From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:02:26 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] webapi: derive iframe layout viewports from their embedding
 element

Window.innerWidth/innerHeight, document client sizes, visualViewport, CSS
viewport units and media queries all used the top-level page viewport in
iframes. This exposed 1100x650 even from zero-size or display:none SDK frames.

Add a Frame viewport derived from the connected embedding element's parent
layout, rounding CSS pixel dimensions like Chrome. Retain page-wide screen,
scale and outer-window metrics. Use 300x150 iframe defaults when no size is
specified, and keep visibility:hidden layout distinct from display:none.

Cache ancestors only within one native layout-query epoch to avoid exponential
work in nested frames while preserving fresh independent reads. Coalesce child
viewport updates on the scheduler after parent style/DOM changes, including
media-query notifications and layout observers; do not invoke JS in getters.

Give each frame its own VisualViewport. Resolve Window, media listener routing,
image/picture selection and SVG length calculations through the receiver or
owning element rather than the caller's realm. Guard MediaQueryList listeners
against a reused or torn-down owner context.

Adjust only the existing iframe media-list renavigation test: width:100vw makes
its page viewport override change the iframe viewport legitimately. No new
upstream tests. All 1658 existing tests pass. Owned isolated Chrome/native
fixtures match seven sizing cases, resize/media/RO delivery, listener removal,
foreign-realm SVG/image reads and a twelve-level frame chain.

The live Sephora SDK exercises innerWidth/innerHeight and its hidden frame now
reports consistent 0x0 window/document/visual dimensions. A normal 60-second
capture still has no binary POST and auth remains 429; this is a real browser
compatibility fix, not an established submission blocker or vendor completion.
Existing faux-layout limitations and remote WebRTC media remain out of scope.
